#{[17592186308587 :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{3x} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =9{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{e}^{3x}-6\\cdot 3{e}^{3x}+9{e}^{3x} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0{\\color{darkgreen}\\hspace{1em}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x-1}$ is a solution to the equation” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{3x-1} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =9{e}^{3x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{e}^{3x-1}-6\\cdot 3{e}^{3x-1}+9{e}^{3x-1} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0{\\color{darkgreen}\\hspace{1em}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation” is false. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{3x} & {e}^{3x-1} \\\\ 3{e}^{3x} & 3{e}^{3x-1}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\cdot 3{e}^{3x-1}-3{e}^{3x}\\cdot {e}^{3x-1} \\\\  & =3{e}^{6x-1}-3{e}^{6x-1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian equals $0$ for all $x\\in \\mathbb{R},$ the functions are not linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation”; “${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x-1}$ is a solution to the equation” only."] [17592186308589 :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =2x \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot 2-3x\\cdot 2x+4{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-6{x}^{2}+4{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0{\\color{darkgreen}\\hspace{1em}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation for $x>0.$” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(2\\operatorname{ln}(x)+3)-3x(2x\\operatorname{ln}(x)+x)+4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}(x) & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}(x)+3{x}^{2}-6{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}(x)-3{x}^{2}+4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}(x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0{\\color{darkgreen}\\hspace{1em}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$” is true. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{x}^{2} & {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) \\\\ 2x & 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x)-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+{x}^{3}-2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian is not identically $0$ on $(0,\\infty ),$ the functions are linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation”; “${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation for $x>0.$”; “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$”."] [17592186308590 :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4\\operatorname{cos}(2x)+4\\operatorname{cos}(2x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0{\\color{darkgreen}\\hspace{1em}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{2}(x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-16\\operatorname{cos}(2x)+16\\operatorname{cos}(2x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0{\\color{darkgreen}\\hspace{1em}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation” is false. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\cdot (-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian equals $0$ for all $x\\in \\mathbb{R},$ the functions are not linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation”; “${y}_{2}(x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation” only."] [17592186337094 :answer/value "{x}^{2}+2\\operatorname{arcsec} x+C"] [17592186348253 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{xy}}\\,{y}^{′}=1$ for $y\\ne 0.$ Work in the region $x>0$ and $y>0$."] [17592186348253 :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{xy}}{y}^{′} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{xy}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\sqrt{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {y}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 2{y}^{1/2} & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n2{y}^{1/2}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2}+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{1/2} & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2}+C \\\\ {y}^{1/2} & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3/2}+\\frac{C}{2} \\\\ y & ={(\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3/2}+\\frac{C}{2})}^{2} \\\\ y & ={(\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3/2}+K)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\frac{C}{2}.$\n\nUse this family on a connected interval where $\\frac{x^{3/2}}3+K>0$. This condition preserves the positive square-root branch used before raising both sides to a power. The calculation above works in the stated positive quadrant; $\\sqrt{xy}=\\sqrt{x}\\sqrt{y}$ is valid there."] [17592186351479 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\sqrt{xy}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1.$ Work in the region $x>0$ and $y>0$."] [17592186351479 :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{xy}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{x}\\sqrt{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\sqrt{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {y}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {x}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3/2} & =2{x}^{1/2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3/2}=2{x}^{1/2}+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3/2} & =2{x}^{1/2}+C \\\\ {y}^{3/2} & =3{x}^{1/2}+\\frac{3C}{2} \\\\ y & ={(3{x}^{1/2}+\\frac{3C}{2})}^{2/3} \\\\ y & ={(3{x}^{1/2}+K)}^{2/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\frac{3C}{2}.$\n\nUse this family on a connected interval where $3\\sqrt{x}+K>0$. This condition preserves the positive square-root branch used before raising both sides to a power. The calculation above works in the stated positive quadrant; $\\sqrt{xy}=\\sqrt{x}\\sqrt{y}$ is valid there."] [17592186357461 :question/problem "Use the transformation $y=v(x)x$ to find the nonzero solution family of the following homogeneous differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}}.\n$$"] [17592186357461 :question/worked-solution "Using the transformation $y=v(x)x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{(vx)^{3}}{x(vx)^{2}+{x}^{3}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{v}^{3}{x}^{3}}{({v}^{2}+1){x}^{3}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{v}^{3}}{{v}^{2}+1} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{v}^{3}}{{v}^{2}+1}-v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{v}^{3}-{v}^{3}-v}{{v}^{2}+1} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{v}{{v}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}+1}{v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{v}^{2}+1}{v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (v+\\frac{1}{v})\\,\\text{d}v & =-\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|v| & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|v| & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+2{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({v}^{2}) & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we introduced $C=2{C}_{1}.$\n\nFrom the transformation $y=vx$, we get\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above into our solution, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡{\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)}^{2} & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\right) & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}) & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}) & =C \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} & =C-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{C-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2})} & ={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe division by $v$ assumes $y\\ne0$. The equilibrium solution $y=0$ also satisfies the original differential equation on any interval with $x\\ne0$; it is not represented by the logarithmic family above."] [17592186373003 :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+{y}^{2}\n$$ that satisfies $y(2)=-1.$\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\"> <tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"bottom\" data-border-top=\"hidden\" data-border-right=\"hidden\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"hidden\"></td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"bottom\" data-border-top=\"hidden\" data-border-bottom=\"hidden\" data-border-left=\"hidden\">$y(x)=$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"bottom\" data-border-top=\"hidden\" data-border-right=\"hidden\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\" data-border-left=\"hidden\">$x$</td> </tr> <tr> <td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"hidden\" data-border-bottom=\"hidden\" data-border-left=\"hidden\"> </td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-bottom=\"hidden\"> </td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-top=\"solid\" data-border-right=\"hidden\" data-border-bottom=\"hidden\" data-border-left=\"hidden\">{{field-1}} </td> </tr> </tbody></table></div>"] [17592186390978 :question/problem "$$\n{\\mathbf{x}}^{'}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\mathbf{x}(t),\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{x}(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{x}(t)={e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2?$"] [17592186390978 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x}(\\operatorname{ln}2) & ={e}^{2(\\operatorname{ln}2)}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{4(\\operatorname{ln}2)}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+16\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 48\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 48\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{x}}^{'}(\\operatorname{ln}2) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 48\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2(4)+0(48) \\\\ 0(4)+4(48)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 192\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186390985 :question/problem "$$\n{\\mathbf{x}}^{'}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\mathbf{x}(t),\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{x}(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{x}(t)={e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3?$"] [17592186390985 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x}(\\operatorname{ln}3) & ={e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{2(\\operatorname{ln}3)}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+9\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -36\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -36\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{x}}^{'}(\\operatorname{ln}3) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -36\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(-1)\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)+0(-36) \\\\ 0\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)+2(-36)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -72\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186390992 :question/problem "$$\n{\\mathbf{x}}^{'}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\mathbf{x}(t),\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{x}(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{x}(t)={e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3?$"] [17592186390992 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x}(\\operatorname{ln}3) & ={e}^{-2(\\operatorname{ln}3)}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{x}}^{'}(\\operatorname{ln}3) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)+0(6) \\\\ 0\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)+1(6)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186391202 :answer/value "zero for all $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$"] [17592186391203 :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$"] [17592186391408 :question/problem "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3}-2x,\\hspace{2em}{y}_{2}(x)=10x-5{x}^{3},\\hspace{2em}x\\in \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}}, which is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $\\mathbb{R}.$"] [17592186391408 :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{x}^{3}-2x & 10x-5{x}^{3} \\\\ ({x}^{3}-2x)^{′} & (10x-5{x}^{3})^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{x}^{3}-2x & 10x-5{x}^{3} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-2 & 10-15{x}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =({x}^{3}-2x)(10-15{x}^{2})-(10x-5{x}^{3})(3{x}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =x({x}^{2}-2)\\cdot 5(2-3{x}^{2})-5x(2-{x}^{2})(3{x}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =5x({x}^{2}-2)(2-3{x}^{2})-5x({x}^{2}-2)(2-3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-15{x}^{5}+40{x}^{3}-20x-(-15{x}^{5}+40{x}^{3}-20x) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{zero for all }x\\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly $\\text{dependent}$ on $\\mathbb{R}.$"] [17592186391410 :answer/value "zero for all $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$"] [17592186391411 :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$"] [17592186391420 :question/problem "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}x,\\hspace{2em}{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}x+1,\\hspace{2em}x\\in \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $\\mathbb{R}.$"] [17592186391420 :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1 \\\\ {(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}^{′} & {(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1)}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-{sin}^{2}⁡x-({cos}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }x\\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $\\mathbb{R}.$"]}
